  ],
  "contradictions": [
    {
      "topic": "Test doubles for substituting collaborators: mocks vs fakes",
      "sourceA": "ahmed",
      "claimA": "Isolate collaborators with Mockk mocks. ViewModel tests declare `@MockK private lateinit var repository` and stub with `coEvery { repository.getItems() } returns ...`; repository tests mock the API the same way (`@MockK ... api`, `coEvery { api.getUser(\"1\") } returns UserDto(...)`). Mock-based isolation is the demonstrated default across the entire skill.",
      "sourceB": "rcosteira",
      "claimB": "Prefer fakes over mocks. android-testing/SKILL.md line 13 names \"fakes over mocks\" as an established foundational principle the skill builds on; chris aligns with this — compose-ui-testing-patterns/SKILL.md line 160 lists \"Testing click behavior through a ViewModel mock\" as a mistake whose fix is \"Pass a callback and assert it was invoked.\"",
      "evidence": "ahmed: mobile-testing/SKILL.md lines 30-31 (`@MockK private lateinit var repository: HomeRepository`) and lines 55-61 (`@MockK private lateinit var api: UserApi` / `coEvery { api.getUser(\"1\") } returns UserDto(\"1\", \"John\")`). rcosteira: android-testing/SKILL.md line 13 (\"...not the basics of the three tiers, fakes over mocks, runTest...\"). chris: compose-ui-testing-patterns/SKILL.md lines 160-161 (mistake table)."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Strictness of test-first (must a failing test always precede production code?)",
      "sourceA": "rcosteira",
      "claimA": "Strict, no exceptions: \"No production code without a failing test first. Write the test, watch it fail (RED)... A behaviour you never watched fail isn't proven.\" A bug isn't fixed until a test that was red because of the bug is green.",
      "sourceB": "ahmed",
      "claimB": "Test-first is optional: tests may be written \"first (TDD) OR alongside your code\" — writing production code before the test is explicitly permitted.",
      "evidence": "rcosteira: android-testing/SKILL.md lines 17-18. ahmed: ios-testing/SKILL.md line 306 (\"Write them first (TDD) or alongside your code, but always maintain them.\"); note ahmed's mobile-testing/SKILL.md line 97 says \"Write tests first\" but the iOS skill relaxes it to \"or alongside.\""
    }
  ],
  "bugs": [
    {
      "source": "chris",
      "file": "/Users/oleksandr.priadko/Documents/StudioProjects/sub/skills/skills/compose-ui-testing-patterns/SKILL.md",
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The interaction-state example launches the `interactionSource.emit(...)` on a fresh, detached `TestScope()` that is never advanced, so the coroutine body never executes and the interaction is never emitted — the example does not do what it claims. `TestScope()` uses a StandardTestDispatcher whose scheduler only runs tasks when explicitly pumped (runCurrent/advanceUntilIdle); the following `composeTestRule.waitForIdle()` synchronizes Compose's own work, not this unrelated TestScope. The subsequent 'assert the visual/semantic change that hover produces' therefore asserts against the un-hovered state. Ironically the skill correctly warns not to use `LaunchedEffect` but substitutes an equally non-working driver.",
      "evidence": "Lines 97-105: `TestScope().launch { interactionSource.emit(HoverInteraction.Enter()) }` followed by `composeTestRule.waitForIdle()` with no scheduler advancement. Correct approaches would emit from the test's own coroutine (e.g. within `runTest`/`runComposeUiTest` and advance) or via `composeTestRule` clock, not a detached TestScope."
    },
    {
      "source": "ahmed",
      "file": "/Users/oleksandr.priadko/Documents/StudioProjects/sub/everything-claude-code-mobile/skills/mobile-testing/SKILL.md",
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The ViewModel test constructs `HomeViewModel(repository)` and drives it with `runTest {}` but installs no MainDispatcherRule / `Dispatchers.setMain(...)`. A standard Android ViewModel launches work in `viewModelScope`, which is bound to `Dispatchers.Main`; without replacing Main in a JVM unit test the test throws `IllegalStateException: Module with the Main dispatcher had failed to initialize`. This is exactly the setup rcosteira's android-testing skill warns about (the two-schedulers trap), and it is absent here.",
      "evidence": "Lines 29-49: `@BeforeEach setup(){ ...; viewModel = HomeViewModel(repository) }` and `fun \\`loads items successfully\\`() = runTest { ... viewModel.onIntent(LoadItems) ... }` with no MainDispatcherRule anywhere in the skill. Contrast rcosteira android-testing/SKILL.md line 26 (pass `mainRule.dispatcher` into `runTest`)."
    },
    {
      "source": "ahmed",
      "file": "/Users/oleksandr.priadko/Documents/StudioProjects/sub/everything-claude-code-mobile/skills/mobile-testing/SKILL.md",
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The Turbine StateFlow test asserts the loading state as the FIRST emission, but `stateFlow.test {}` replays the current (initial) value first. `onIntent(LoadItems)` is called before the first `awaitItem()`, and the initial state (isLoading almost certainly false) is what the first `awaitItem()` returns, so `awaitItem().isLoading shouldBe true` asserts against the wrong item. Correct pattern is to consume/skip the initial item first, then trigger the action; with StandardTestDispatcher the loading emission may also be conflated away without an explicit advance.",
      "evidence": "Lines 43-47: `viewModel.state.test { viewModel.onIntent(LoadItems); awaitItem().isLoading shouldBe true; awaitItem().items shouldBe listOf(item) }` — no initial `awaitItem()`/`skipItems(1)` before triggering the intent."
    },
    {
      "source": "ahmed",
      "file": "/Users/oleksandr.priadko/Documents/StudioProjects/sub/everything-claude-code-mobile/skills/mobile-verification/SKILL.md",
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The Espresso flaky-fix snippet uses `@IdlingResource` as an annotation on a property, but there is no such annotation — `androidx.test.espresso.IdlingResource` is an interface. The snippet will not compile and teaches a nonexistent API; a `CountingIdlingResource` is used by registering it via `IdlingRegistry.getInstance().register(...)`, not by annotating a field. `@get:Rule val disableAnimationsRule = DisableAnimationsRule()` also references a non-standard/undefined class.",
      "evidence": "Lines 116-124: `@IdlingResource val countingIdlingResource = CountingIdlingResource(\"api\")` and `@get:Rule val disableAnimationsRule = DisableAnimationsRule()`."
    },
    {
      "source": "ahmed",
      "file": "/Users/oleksandr.priadko/Documents/StudioProjects/sub/everything-claude-code-mobile/skills/mobile-verification/SKILL.md",
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Presents wrapping content in `CompositionLocalProvider(LocalInspectionMode provides true)` as the fix strategy for Compose test flakiness whose listed causes are 'Recomposition timing / State hoisting issues / Animation interference.' `LocalInspectionMode` is a preview/inspection flag; it does not control recomposition timing, state hoisting, or the test clock, so it is not a remedy for those flake causes. The correct levers (per rcosteira) are `mainClock.autoAdvance=false` before setContent, `advanceTimeByFrame()`, and `mainClock.advanceTimeUntil { ... }`.",
      "evidence": "Lines 135-150: under 'Compose Tests ... Common Flaky Causes: Recomposition timing / State hoisting issues / Animation interference', the 'Fix Strategies' block is `CompositionLocalProvider(LocalInspectionMode provides true){ content() }`."
    }
  ],
  "depthComparison": "For the Android testing+debugging topic, rcosteira is the most detailed, accurate, and actionable — and it is the only source with any debugging coverage at all. android-testing/SKILL.md nails the modern Android test traps precisely: Compose tests now defaulting to StandardTestDispatcher (with the exact ComposeUiTestFlags gate and how to pin a scheduler), the two-schedulers MainDispatcherRule-vs-runTest trap, test-clock vs wall-clock (mainClock.advanceTimeUntil vs rule.waitUntil), and animation determinism (mainClock.autoAdvance=false before setContent, the InfiniteAnimationPolicy CancellationException, advanceTimeByFrame). android-debugging/SKILL.md is likewise deep and correct across Logcat/ADB, ANR traces, LeakCanary/heap dumps, Perfetto capture+SQL, R8 retrace/mapping files, a Gradle-failure triage table, `android layout` runtime inspection, and Compose recomposition (with an up-to-date strong-skipping-mode note). chris (compose-ui-testing-patterns) is the deepest on the narrow slice of Compose UI-test design — test-target-choice matrix, semantics-first, interaction-state testing, screenshot determinism, and a mistakes/red-flags review checklist — though its flagship MutableInteractionSource example is broken (see bugs). ahmed's three skills are the broadest in surface area (Android unit/Compose, full iOS/XCTest, and a pass@k verification methodology) but the shallowest and least reliable per line: mock-heavy examples that omit the MainDispatcherRule, a non-compiling `@IdlingResource` snippet, and a mis-applied LocalInspectionMode 'fix'. Net: rcosteira dominates on correctness and breadth for Android; chris wins the Compose-UI-test niche; ahmed's real value is coverage the others lack (iOS, dependency/versions, flakiness/pass@k) rather than depth.",
  "uniqueValue": [
    {
      "source": "ahmed:mobile-testing",
      "whatIsUnique": "The only source that gives a concrete Android test dependency stack with pinned versions (JUnit5 junit-jupiter 5.10.0, Mockk 1.13.8, Turbine 1.0.0, kotlinx-coroutines-test, Kotest assertions) plus a Turbine StateFlow ViewModel-testing pattern and coverage tooling (Kover `./gradlew koverHtmlReport`, 80% target). No other file specifies dependencies, versions, or coverage measurement."
    },
    {
      "source": "ahmed:ios-testing",
      "whatIsUnique": "Entire iOS/Swift/SwiftUI/XCTest testing surface — XCTestCase setUp/tearDown, protocol-based mocking, async/await + XCTestExpectation, XCUIApplication UI tests, snapshot testing, ViewInspector view tests, performance measure{}, and a test-folder organization layout. No other source covers iOS at all; valuable cross-platform coverage for the merged plugin."
    },
    {
      "source": "ahmed:mobile-verification",
      "whatIsUnique": "A pass@k reliability/flaky-detection methodology found nowhere else: running tests k times (k=2/3/5/10 tiers), pass@k thresholds per test type (unit 0.95, UI 0.80, Compose 0.90, integration 0.70, E2E 0.60), flaky-pattern-to-cause table, trend tracking, and release-gating, plus integration hooks (checkpoints/memory/instincts). No other source quantifies flakiness or gates releases on repeated runs."
    },
    {
      "source": "chris:compose-ui-testing-patterns",
      "whatIsUnique": "Interaction-state testing via injected MutableInteractionSource (emit Hover/Press/Focus/Drag interactions and assert the resulting visual/semantic change) with an explicit rule against simulating pointer/mouse events — a technique absent from every other file. Also unique: a test-target-choice decision matrix, a fake-image-loader pattern for asserting requested models, and a structured mistakes/red-flags-during-review checklist."
    },
    {
      "source": "rcosteira:android-testing",
      "whatIsUnique": "The StandardTestDispatcher-as-new-Compose-test-default explanation (ComposeUiTestFlags.isStandardTestDispatcherSupportEnabled gate; effectContext pinning); the two-schedulers trap (share MainDispatcherRule's dispatcher into runTest); test-clock vs wall-clock guidance (mainClock.advanceTimeUntil vs rule.waitUntil as a flake source); animation determinism (autoAdvance=false before setContent, InfiniteAnimationPolicy CancellationException, advanceTimeByFrame); Coil3 FakeImageLoader; and pointers to Google testing-setup + the skydoves tag-first counterview with the material3 testTag ratio."
    },
    {
      "source": "rcosteira:android-debugging",
      "whatIsUnique": "The ONLY debugging skill in the set — zero overlap with any other file. Covers Logcat/ADB filtering by pidof, ANR trace analysis (/data/anr/traces.txt, main-thread MONITOR state), LeakCanary + manual heap dumps, Perfetto trace capture + SQL, R8/ProGuard retrace with mapping/usage/seeds files and jadx --deobf for third-party traces, a Gradle build-failure triage table, `android layout` runtime UI-tree inspection, Compose recomposition debugging, and a multi-component unique-per-session-tag instrumentation template with grep-based teardown."
    }
  ]
}

For each contradiction: is it a REAL opposing-advice conflict, or just different depth/context/wording of the same principle? Verdict CONFIRMED | OVERTURNED | PARTIAL, with reasoning citing the actual lines.
For each bug: re-read the cited code. Is it actually broken/wrong in a way that matters? Verdict CONFIRMED | OVERTURNED | PARTIAL.
Also list missedIssues: any real contradiction or bug the finder MISSED that you noticed.

Default to OVERTURNED when you cannot find clear evidence in the files. Be skeptical and specific.
